    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born out of wedlock near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and trained in the workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio, Leonardo made his career at the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, Florence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a painter, engineer and deviser of pageants.</li><li>Fewer than 25 major works are attributed to him, several of them unfinished or ruined by his own experiments in technique, yet the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper are among the most famous and most reproduced paintings in the Western canon.</li><li>His notebooks record discoveries in anatomy, hydrodynamics, geology, optics and friction, but he did not publish them, and they had little to no direct influence on later science.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Most of Leonardo's writings are in mirror-image cursive. He wrote with his left hand, and it was probably easier for him to write from right to left.</p></aside>
    </section>

